Z||000018||GoCartGuy||03-18-2001||01:41 AM||||That's the right idea, just go buy a square rod of aluminum or steel or whatever at a hardware store.  Time to plug the job "Lowe's" is a good choice.  Slide it into the plug and clamp a set of vice-grips on the other end to turn it with.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000019||voyagerspe||03-18-2001||10:05 AM||||There is a proper tool to get the plug out.  <BR>however you dont want to buy it for one use.<BR>it looks like a torx socket but its impact.  I went to a local engine builder/machine shop<BR>he took the plug out in about 30 seconds.  He told me the "socket" costs over $150.00 I gave him $5.00 and he was happy.  Oh, and by the way I worked on this plug for about 3 hours trying to get it out and ruined a 3/8 extention (i ground it down to fit).  Just find a machine shop and let them do it.||||reg||1
